The property is **`tokenizerId`**. In that test (in `packages/core/test/assemble.test.ts`), the simulator is constructed as:

```ts
const bytes = new ProviderCacheSimulator({
  tokenizer: new ExactTokenizer('bytes-v0', (text) => text.length),
  ...
});
```

and asserted with:

```ts
expect(bytes.tokenizerId).toBe('bytes-v0');
```

That check sits alongside the other assertions in the same test: `bytes.total` must *not* equal `heuristic.total` (the byte-exact tokenizer produces different token counts than the heuristic one), while `bytes.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds)` must equal `heuristic.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds)` — i.e., the two simulators segment the prompt into the same cacheable block groups even though their token accounting differs. The `tokenizerId` expectation pins that the `ExactTokenizer` really is the `'bytes-v0'` implementation under test (the id it reports through `ProviderCacheSimulator`), not silently fallen back to the heuristic tokenizer.

