So `'bytes-v0'` is the tokenizer ID carried on the simulator instance itself (`bytes.tokenizerId`), reflecting the `ExactTokenizer` it was constructed with. The rest of the test then asserts the structural equivalence between the two simulators — `bytes.total` must *differ* from `heuristic.total` (the tokenizer is exact, so the totals shouldn't match the heuristic approximation), while `bytes.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds)` must *equal* `heuristic.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds)` — i.e., the segmentation is identical even though the token counts differ.

